    "why": "When the Constitution was written, only white men who owned property could vote in most places. The right to vote has been expanded four times by amendment — for race, for women, against poll taxes, and for 18-year-olds. Each one made America a little more democratic. The work of including everyone took almost 200 years.",
    "fun": "The 26th Amendment was ratified in just 100 days — faster than any other amendment in U.S. history. The push came from the Vietnam War: 18-year-olds were being drafted to fight but couldn't vote for the leaders who sent them. The slogan was 'old enough to fight, old enough to vote.' States agreed in record time.",
    "src": "archives.gov",
